<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nalanda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nalanda was a great Buddhist monastery and centre of learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, which flourished from the 5th to the 12th century. Supported by the Gupta kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> and the Pala Empire, it drew students from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, including the Chinese pilgrims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a>. It declined after attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a>'s forces around 1200, and its ruins are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nalanda.jpg" alt="Nalanda" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Odantapuribs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_No.-_3,_Nalanda_Archaeological_Site.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nalanda lies in the ancient region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, about 16 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajgir</a> and about 90 kilometres southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>. Bodh Gaya, another major Buddhist site, is about 80 kilometres to the southwest. The site lies between the artificial lakes of Gidhi, Panashokar and Indrapuskarani. Excavations at the nearby Juafardih site have yielded black ware carbon-dated to about 1200 BCE, so the region was settled long before the Buddha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its datable history begins in the 5th century. A seal names a king called Shakraditya as founder of a monastery there, and scholars identify him with the Gupta emperor Kumaragupta I, whose coins found at the site support this. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, who visited around 637, said that a long succession of kings had built up Nalanda. The Gupta patrons were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, the emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, also supported it. Under the Buddhist Pala dynasty, which reigned from the mid-8th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> tradition of Nalanda gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana</a>, and the Palas built competing monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a> and Odantapura, only a few kilometres away, which probably drew monks from Nalanda.</p><p>Excavations in 1920 and 1921 found a thick layer of ash in the upper levels, which has been blamed on the troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji</a>, who plundered the region around 1200. The passage often cited, in the Tabaqat-i Nasiri, actually describes the destruction of Odantapura, though it is true that Nalanda was raided. The scholar Johan Elverskog argues that the end was not sudden: students were still there in the 13th century, and a monk ordained there travelled to the court of Khubilai Khan. After its decline the site was forgotten. Francis Buchanan-Hamilton surveyed it in 1811 and 1812, and Major Markham Kittoe identified it as Nalanda in 1847. Systematic excavation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> ran from 1915 to 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The curriculum covered Madhyamaka, Yogachara and other Buddhist philosophies, and also the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, logic, grammar, medicine and Samkhya. Pupils came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Yijing, who spent ten years there, took home many texts, which shows a well-equipped library; Tibetan sources name it Dharmaganja and describe three multi-storeyed buildings. Yijing also records that the monks decided matters in assembly, and that a single objection stopped a proposal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shantarakshita">Shantarakshita</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a>, who helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in Tibet, came from Nalanda. Its ruins were listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nalanda mahavihara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conquest also brought destruction: temples were demolished and their materials reused, as in the Quwwat al-Islam mosque begun at Delhi in the 1190s, and Bakhtiyar Khalji's raids in Bihar are traditionally associated with the sack of Buddhist monasteries such as <b>Nalanda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Balaputra, displaced in Java, became Maharaja of Srivijaya and in his <b>Nalanda</b> inscription of 860 claimed the Shailendra inheritance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Devapala granted five villages to support a monastery at <b>Nalanda</b> built by Balaputradeva, the Shailendra king of Suvarnadvipa (Sumatra), at that king’s request.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Srivijayan rulers also endowed Buddhist foundations abroad, including a monastery at <b>Nalanda</b> in Bihar and the Chudamani Vihara at Nagapattinam on the Chola coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal patronage shifted to Hindu devotional traditions, and the great monastic universities in the east, which depended on that patronage, were sacked during the Turkic conquests around 1200; <b>Nalanda</b> was among them.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The great Buddhist monastic university of <b>Nalanda</b> taught in Sanskrit.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
